    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get that. so if those inharmonic overtones or those \"unexpected\" sounds are what create that roughness, how do they actually change the mathematical relationship? like, does it mess with the whole harmonic series structure if those notes are fighting the fundamental?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When inharmonic overtones are introduced into the sound of an instrument, they disrupt the simple mathematical relationship of a harmonic series, which is based on integer multiples of the fundamental frequency. The harmonic series is essentially a perfect theoretically based framework because the overtone frequencies are theoretically tuned to these integer multiples. However, in practice, a multitude of factors—including the physical properties of the sound source, air pressure, and nonlinear effects—can cause the overtone frequencies to deviate from these integer multiples, hence resulting in inharmonic overtones.\n\n\nThese inharmonic overtones arise due to various reasons, such as the physical deformation of the instrument, the stiffness of the material, or interactions between different parts of the instrument's sound production system, such as the dual resonance of a tuba-like body with its slides. Mathematically, the inharmonicity can be represented by a correction factor applied to the ideal harmonic series. This factor alters the harmonic frequencies away from integer multiples of the fundamental, leading to a non-harmonic overtone series. This results in a complex and richer sound, but one that can slightly complicate the harmonic structure that a listener might associate with the purely harmonic series.",
      "source": "target",
